% ============================================================================
% sound-properties — each PERCEIVED property of a sound and the PHYSICAL
% quantity of the sound wave it corresponds to (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of acoustics, and a rung a PHYSICS (or a MUSIC,
% or a HEARING/audiology) agent reasons UP from: what your EAR hears is not the
% same vocabulary as what a physicist MEASURES, and the two vocabularies line up
% one-for-one. What you hear as PITCH is the wave's FREQUENCY; what you hear as
% LOUDNESS is the wave's AMPLITUDE; what you hear as TIMBRE (why a violin and a
% flute playing the same note still sound different) is the wave's WAVEFORM —
% the shape of the wave. Before you can reason about a tone, an instrument, or a
% hearing test you must first know WHICH physical quantity a heard property maps
% to. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? sound_property(pitch, $Physical)      % frequency
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `sound_property(perceived, physical)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% physical quantity WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these three perceived properties (it
% never invents a correspondence).
%
% Each perceived property and the physical quantity the source states it
% corresponds to, as a little truth table:
%
%     perceived (what you hear)   physical (what is measured)   a beginner's cue
%     -------------------------   ---------------------------   ----------------
%     pitch                       frequency                     high note = fast wave
%     loudness                    amplitude                     loud = tall wave
%     timbre                      waveform                      violin vs. flute = wave SHAPE
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a physical quantity and recall the
% perceived property it is heard as:
%
%     ? sound_property($Perceived, frequency)   % pitch
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three perceived properties — `rhythm`,
% `duration`, `color` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a sound-property recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a correspondence. That honest-abstention
% property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers
% exactly the perceived->physical pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `physical` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, and each one is
% a word that appears LITERALLY in the quoted source span below — the source
% says the three heard qualities correspond to the wave's "fundamental
% frequency, amplitude and waveform, respectively", so the atoms are
% `frequency`, `amplitude`, and `waveform`, each checkable against that span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every perceived->physical
% pair below was read out of a fetched source, and any pair that could not be
% verified there would have been dropped).
%
% The table's single provenance envelope holds one clean span that fixes ALL
% THREE rows at once — a Physics LibreTexts conceptual-physics course page:
%
%   pitch -> frequency, loudness -> amplitude, timbre -> waveform
%     https://phys.libretexts.org/Courses/Joliet_Junior_College/Physics_110_-_by_Conceptual_Objective/12:_Conceptual_Objective_12/12.06:_Pitch_Loudness_and_Timbre
%     (Physics LibreTexts — a teaching summary, so `trust consensus`)
%     "The three subjective quantities of pitch, loudness and timbre are
%      related to laboratory measurements of a sound wave's fundamental
%      frequency, amplitude and waveform, respectively."
%
% The first two rows (pitch -> frequency, loudness -> amplitude) are ALSO
% corroborated by a primary U.S. government / academic source — the NIH NCBI
% Bookshelf edition of Purves et al., *Neuroscience* (chapter "Sound"):
%
%     https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11126/
%     (NIH NCBI Bookshelf — authoritative)
%     "For the human listener, the amplitude and frequency of a sound roughly
%      correspond to loudness and pitch, respectively."
%
% That NIH sentence does NOT name timbre, so it grounds only two of the three
% rows; the timbre -> waveform row rests on the LibreTexts span, which is why the
% envelope uses the single LibreTexts sentence (it fixes all three rows) and the
% table's `trust` is honestly `consensus`, not `authoritative`. No row was
% invented: the timbre -> waveform row is included ONLY because the LibreTexts
% source explicitly states it.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table sound_property {
    columns perceived, physical

    row (pitch, frequency)
    row (loudness, amplitude)
    row (timbre, waveform)

    source "The three subjective quantities of pitch, loudness and timbre are related to laboratory measurements of a sound wave's fundamental frequency, amplitude and waveform, respectively."
    locator "https://phys.libretexts.org/Courses/Joliet_Junior_College/Physics_110_-_by_Conceptual_Objective/12:_Conceptual_Objective_12/12.06:_Pitch_Loudness_and_Timbre"
    trust consensus
}
